  "narrative": {
    "intro": "HUBZone set-aside obligations totaled $482M in FY2026 to date. The program produced 1,352 awards with an average award size of $356K. Obligations are down 47.6% year over year. Yearly comparisons show obligations of $938M in FY21, $1B in FY22, $1.1B in FY23, $1B in FY24, $920M in FY25, and $482M in FY26. The Department of Defense obligated $169M, making it the largest buying agency for HUBZone set-asides in FY2026 to date. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $84.3M and the Department of Labor obligated $54.9M. Other notable agencies include the Department of Transportation at $36.3M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $31.6M. Other Computer Related Services is the largest industry by obligations at $125M, followed by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $49.3M and Facilities Support Services at $46.4M. T&H SERVICES, LLC is the top vendor with $30.2M in obligations, followed by ANACAPA MICRO PRODUCTS, INC. at $20.7M and PANAMERICA COMPUTERS, INC. at $19.5M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has HUBZone funding changed over recent years?",
        "body": "Fiscal-year obligations rose from $938M in FY21 to $1.1B in FY23, then leveled at $1B in FY24 and fell to $920M in FY25. Obligations are $482M in FY26 to date, and they are down 47.6% year over year. Award volume for FY26 to date is 1,352 awards with an average award size of $356K. The recent drop in FY26 is visible when comparing the current total to prior fiscal years."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries are the biggest users of HUBZone set-asides?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $169M in obligations, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $84.3M and the Department of Labor at $54.9M. Other Computer Related Services leads industries with $125M in obligations, followed by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $49.3M and Facilities Support Services at $46.4M. T&H SERVICES, LLC is the top vendor with $30.2M, and ANACAPA MICRO PRODUCTS, INC. and PANAMERICA COMPUTERS, INC. follow with $20.7M and $19.5M respectively."
      }
    ]
  },
